WEBVTT

1-1
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.815
Your stack is awesome, but you need a way to manage it.

1-2
00:00:02.935 --> 00:00:08.079
StackOps is your OS-agnostic digital life manager. It covers 99% of your digital footprint.

1-3
00:00:08.199 --> 00:00:09.459
The applications you use.

1-4
00:00:09.579 --> 00:00:10.792
The way you configure them.

1-5
00:00:10.912 --> 00:00:12.592
Your secrets and credentials.

1-6
00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:14.239
Your files and data.

1-7
00:00:14.359 --> 00:00:15.706
Your code and repositories.

1-8
00:00:15.826 --> 00:00:17.312
And the processes you run.

1-9
00:00:17.432 --> 00:00:19.119
All wrapped into one solution.

2-1
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:21.678
Start with a fresh, bare machine.

2-2
00:00:21.798 --> 00:00:24.770
Now bring back the environment you've spent ten years making your own.

2-3
00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:29.270
That's the goal: one command, five minutes, and your familiar digital life ready to use.

3-1
00:00:30.093 --> 00:00:35.411
Literally, you can install and configure your entire dev environment while casually inspecting a machine in the store.

3-2
00:00:35.531 --> 00:00:38.603
By the time they notice you are doing something unusual, your setup is done.

3-3
00:00:38.723 --> 00:00:42.661
This config is based on public setup, and ignores private setup and files.

4-1
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:53.222
StackOps doesn't reinvent the wheel. It glues together the best open-source tools. It's a configurator, a package manager, a dotfile manager, a data sync solution, a digital life manager.

5-1
00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.773
One CLI covers setup, cloud, terminals, agents, and everyday tools.

5-2
00:00:57.893 --> 00:01:01.538
DevOps handles installation, configuration, networking, and security.

5-3
00:01:01.658 --> 00:01:05.461
Restore public and private settings, dotfiles, and terminal profiles.

5-4
00:01:05.581 --> 00:01:08.023
Back up, sync, and retrieve your repositories and data.

5-5
00:01:08.143 --> 00:01:10.711
Launch workspaces and orchestrate everyday processes.

6-1
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:13.807
That's StackOps. Your digital life, sorted.

6-2
00:01:13.927 --> 00:01:17.882
Think of it as a Dockerfile: a recipe for how to reproduce your computer.
